  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Waldron bought them six innings after the starter crapped out. The problem is they couldn't find anything until Rodriguez answered.",
    "what_this_means": "The bullpen grind is real. After Rodriguez lasted 1.0 IP, Matt Waldron absorbed the damage: 2.2 innings, six earned runs. The game got away in the fourth when Milwaukee scored five. Wandy Peralta stopped the bleeding. Two scoreless innings, two strikeouts. Yuki Matsui finished clean. The offense scraped together eight hits and 13 strikeouts. Miguel Andujar carried them with a home run and two RBI, converting just two of six RISP situations. They had a second-inning tie and never led again. Milwaukee finished them 6\u20134. This is what a fragile roster looks like when the starter exits early and the lineup can't compensate. They've lost their last game after winning two straight. ERA climbed from 4.07 to 4.13. They're still in first in the division, but the margin isn't getting wider. This is how the cushion erodes. One inning at a time, one failure to execute when runners are on base.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jacob Misiorowski's 2.45 ERA makes him the story tonight. Michael King's 2.74 mark over his last four starts is sound enough, but he's pitching into a lineup that just proved it struggles with fastballs early and can't manufacture runs later. One mistake becomes a two-run deficit they chase all night.",
